  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 150 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThursday through Monday:\n\nSynopsis: Post-frontal surface flow will allow modestly drier air to \nsettle into southern Wisconsin on Thursday. The break in humidity \nwill be short-lived, however, as subtropical high pressure lingering \noff the Mid-Atlantic coast allows large-scale flow to shift south-\nsouthwesterly regionally into the weekend. Thus anticipate \nincreasing dew points & humidity regionally from Friday through at \nleast Sunday. Increasing humidity will be accompanied by a northward-\nreturning warm front, which is forecast to stall somewhere from the \nNorthern Plains to the western Great Lakes by Sunday. Arrival of the \nboundary, along with increasing zonal flow in the upper levels, will \nthus open the door for periods of thunderstorms Saturday night \nthrough the conclusion of the long term. Batches of storms will be \naccompanied by windows of dry conditions, with all-day washouts not \nanticipated. While long lead-time makes it difficult to offer \nprecise details, building humidity & increasing winds aloft could \nsupport some potential for more organized/stronger storms with heavy \ndownpours during the Saturday night-Monday time frame. Will be \nmonitoring trends through this week and providing additional \ninformation as details become clearer.  \n\nSaturday night through Monday: With a quasi-stationary surface front \nin the vicinity & increasing jet dynamics aloft, the broad pattern \nwill become favorable for periods of thunderstorms across the \nwestern Great Lakes. Relative stability of the large-scale pattern--\n-namely the quasi-stationary nature of the surface front & a\n flat/static height field in the upper levels---points toward\n multiple batches of storm development through this portion of\n the period. Where exactly each batch of development occurs will\n depend on placement of the surface front, the nose of the low\n level jet, and smaller scale features stemming from prior\n periods of storm activity. Expect some of these details to\n clarify by the end of the week. Building instability/moisture &\n increasing wind shear point toward some broad potential for\n more organized storms with heavy downpours, though finer\n details won't become clearer until later in the week. Stay\n tuned to later forecast updates. In addition to storm\n potential, do expect heat indices to become elevated once more\n during this time period. Whether/not heat headlines will become\n necessary remains uncertain, as clouds/storms could interfere\n with heat indices being maximized.\n\nQuigley",
